    <h1>Ravens in Japanese Mythology</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Yatagarasu — the three-legged divine crow</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <strong>Yatagarasu</strong> (八咫烏) is a three-legged divine crow or raven of Japanese mythology, sent by Amaterasu to guide Emperor Jimmu to the Yamato region. It appears in the <em>Kojiki</em> (712 CE) and <em>Nihon Shoki</em> (720 CE) as a symbol of divine will and guidance — a strongly positive corvid figure, opposite to the ominous Western tradition. Its three legs are linked in some interpretations to the three virtues of wisdom, benevolence, and courage.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Primary Sources</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The two oldest extant Japanese chronicles both record Yatagarasu:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><em>Kojiki</em></strong> (712 CE, "Record of Ancient Matters"): compiled by Ō no Yasumaro under imperial order; contains the account of Jimmu's expedition to Yamato and the divine crow guide</li>
      <li><strong><em>Nihon Shoki</em></strong> (720 CE, "Chronicles of Japan"): the second official chronicle; also records the Yatagarasu story with some variation</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both texts were compiled in the 8th century CE but record mythological traditions of uncertain antiquity. The <em>Kojiki</em> is the primary narrative source; the <em>Nihon Shoki</em> provides alternative versions.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Story of Yatagarasu</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In the <em>Kojiki</em> narrative:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Emperor Jimmu, leading his forces from Hyūga (Kyushu) toward the east, became lost in the mountains of Yoshino</li>
      <li>Amaterasu, the sun goddess, commanded Takemikazuchi-no-kami to guide Jimmu; in some versions, the gods sent Yatagarasu as the guide</li>
      <li>The great crow led Jimmu's forces through the wilderness to the Yamato region (present-day Nara Prefecture) — the future heartland of the imperial state</li>
      <li>Yatagarasu's role is as a divine messenger and guide, not an omen of death</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The name Yatagarasu: <em>ya</em> (八) = eight; <em>ta</em> (咫) = an old unit of measurement (the width of a spread hand); <em>garasu</em> (烏) = crow/raven. "Eight-span crow" — indicating great size.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Three Legs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Yatagarasu is depicted with three legs — a feature shared with the sun-crow (<em>sanzuwu</em> or <em>sānzúwū</em>) of Chinese mythology, which also has three legs and is associated with the sun:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The three-legged crow appears in Chinese sources from at least the Han dynasty (206 BCE–220 CE) — it was thought to live in the sun</li>
      <li>Japanese and Chinese mythological traditions were in contact through cultural exchange; the three-legged sun-crow may have been adopted and transformed in Japan</li>
      <li>Various later Japanese interpretations of the three legs include associations with Amaterasu, Tsukuyomi, and Susanoo (the sun, moon, and sea deities); or with wisdom, benevolence, and courage</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The connection between the Chinese three-legged sun-crow and Yatagarasu is discussed by scholars but the precise transmission is not fully documented in the sources.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Yatagarasu in Modern Japan</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Yatagarasu remains a living symbol in Japanese culture:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>Japan Football Association (JFA)</strong> adopted Yatagarasu as its emblem in 1931 — the three-legged crow appears on the national team kit</li>
      <li>Kumano Grand Shrine (Kumano Taisha) in Wakayama Prefecture is closely associated with Yatagarasu — the region is traditionally linked to the Jimmu expedition, and crow imagery is prominent in Kumano religious contexts</li>
      <li>Kumano Nachi Taisha also uses a Yatagarasu emblem</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The football association connection makes Yatagarasu one of the most globally recognised Japanese mythological figures outside Japan, through the visibility of the Japan national football team.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Crow or Raven?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> <em>Karasu</em> (烏) is the Japanese word used for both crows and ravens — it does not distinguish between species. The Jungle Crow (<em>Corvus macrorhynchos</em>) is the most familiar large corvid in Japan and is often what Japanese speakers visualise. The Large-billed Crow is a large, impressive bird well-suited to divine messenger imagery. The Common Raven does not occur in Japan's main islands.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
  </section>

